Or both.

> (I guess you could also look at the rknown field to
> tell the two situations apart.

Wouldn't at least one of the individual cases (it's
been worn or it's been identified) be indistinguishable
from the unified case (wherein it's been both worn
and formally identified)?

That's not an unusual scenario, for two reasons.

First, if the player reads enough blessed scrolls
of identify, sooner or later everything in inventory
gets formally identified, including worn items.  I
think thrones can do this too, although it's not
nearly as likely as with the scrolls.

Second, certain kinds of magical armor are often
identified before wearing, to guard against the
possibility that they might be something dangerous
(e.g., a helm of opposite alignment, which would kill
all your protection), self-cursing (duncecap), or
otherwise undesirable.

A player might identify a visored helm, find out
it's a helm of telepathy, wear it for a while, and
later ditch it for a helm of brilliance (or for an
elven leather helm if they need to not have any
metal on for spellcasting reasons).

Also, players who can cast the identify spell
and don't currently have a pet handy will identify
an object just to determine whether it's cursed.
By the time you could traverse several levels
to the nearest altar your power has long since
regenerated (typically).  This is especially
common in the early middle game, when the
player's outfit is not yet finalized.

So I don't think it's reasonable to assume that
has-been-worn and has-been-formally-identified
are mutually exclusive possibilities.  They're
orthogonal:  an item can be either, neither,
or both.

Admittedly, if the player knows the item's
beatitude and enchantment, I'm not sure I
can think of a situation where it would matter
very much exactly how they'd found out.
I just sort of took the original poster at his
word about having a reason to want to be
able to determine this.  Who knows?  Maybe
he wants to write a patch that calls an item
"your blessed +0 orcish helm" (or whatever)
if it's the one you were previously wearing,
so you can easily recognize when you get
back things that nymphs have stolen or
want to put back on the stuff a foocubus
removed.


